ymmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/state.json" "file_sha256" "bb02ea64c6406af5947f6735f7e3abbac6f894588bff7df58b5c9ec8c49771ff" "basis" 1232 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\sqrt[5]{(-x)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[5]{-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/state.json" "file_sha256" "bb02ea64c6406af5947f6735f7e3abbac6f894588bff7df58b5c9ec8c49771ff" "basis" 1232 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-334359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9c1ec128486d1779cde54681fee1a120288070b2589fe0b7e6859e2636d19e74" "basis" 1232 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033652/edb-import/verification.json"} {"