ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c26f7d5a82b9903e2fa8a28011835999e0a48010f9f38ef2da2f550b8d7db72" "basis" 2070 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-156182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c26f7d5a82b9903e2fa8a28011835999e0a48010f9f38ef2da2f550b8d7db72" "basis" 2070 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-156182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x){e}^{(-x)^{4}} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c26f7d5a82b9903e2fa8a28011835999e0a48010f9f38ef2da2f550b8d7db72" "basis" 2070 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" 