ce/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056630/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-88692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069515/state.json" "file_sha256" "5a8005b8b65c17e2d931ccbe4d8430df5d97a958094afe6ac4bdad0a4b51b3e5" "basis" 2387 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069515/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-88692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)({e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)}) \\\\  & =-x({e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069515/state.json" "file_sha256" "5a8005b8b65c17e2d931ccbe4d8430df5d97a958094afe6ac4bdad0a4b51b3e5" "basis" 2387 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069515/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-88692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069515/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f8fc0877b95a6e04805946e97e9ff9bac9760ccd18322977ff35d8497af8be98" "basis" 2387 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069515/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-88692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069515/state.json" "file_sha256" "5a8005b8b65c17e2d931ccbe4d8430df5d97a958094afe6ac4bdad0a4b51b3e5" "basis" 2387 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069515/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-88692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069515/state.json" "file_sha256" "5a8005b8b65c17e2d931ccbe4d8430df5d97a958094afe6ac4bdad0a4b51b3e5" "basis" 2387 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069515/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-88692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069515/state.json" "file_sha256" "5a8005b8b65c17e2d931ccbe4d8430df5d97a958094afe6ac4bdad0a4b51b3e5" "basis" 2387 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069515/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-88692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069515/state.json" "file_sha256" "5a8005b8b65c17e2d931ccbe4d8430df5d97a958094afe6ac4bdad0a4b51b3e5" "basis" 2387 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069515/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-88692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069515/state.json" "file_sha256" "5a8005b8b65c17e2d931ccbe4d8430df5d97a958094afe6ac4bdad0a4b51b3e5" "basis" 2387 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069515/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-88692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069515/state.json" "file_sha256" "5a8005b8b65c17e2d931ccbe4d8430df5d97a958094afe6ac4bdad0a4b51b3e5" "basis" 2387 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069515/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-88209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{6}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-9}^{9}2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-88209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3(-x)^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2} \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=5{x}^{3}$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2{x}^{5}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =2(-x)^{5} \\\\  & =-2{x}^{5} \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-9}^{9}2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-88209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3108668edb4f10359451b5de09a9925181898d10b3f13e55cfdd52068abdcb63"} {"question" "q-88209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-88209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-88209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-88692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-88692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)({e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)}) \\\\  & =-x({e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-88692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3108668edb4f10359451b5de09a9925181898d10b3f13e55cfdd52068abdcb63"} {"question" "q-88692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-88692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-88692" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-156182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-156182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x){e}^{(-x)^{4}} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-156182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3108668edb4f10359451b5de09a9925181898d10b3f13e55cfdd52068abdcb63"} {"question" "q-156182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-156182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "-{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "4{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-156182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-156183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-156183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =({2}^{(-x)}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{(-x)}}) \\\\  & =({(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-\\frac{1}{(1/2)^{x}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}}-{2}^{x}) \\\\  & =-({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}}) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-156183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3108668edb4f10359451b5de09a9925181898d10b3f13e55cfdd52068abdcb63"} {"question" "q-156183" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-156183" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{\\int }_{0}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-156183" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-334359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-334359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\sqrt[5]{(-x)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[5]{-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-334359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3108668edb4f10359451b5de09a9925181898d10b3f13e55cfdd52068abdcb63"} {"question" "q-334359" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072408/state.json" "file_sha256" "36a107cb50c4b14e0a86cf90bbd6b628b2838dd154f672072565eb14ffa6b4b2"} {"question" "q-334359" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/ref